The following information is being provided to as a means of helping those who
are testing the OS/2 version of PCBoard to catch up with the changes that have
been made and to aid in setting it up.

- As a standard, the naming conventions for our products, PCBoard-for-DOS and
  PCBoard-for-OS/2, may be abbreviated as PCB-DOS and PCB-OS2.

- See the NEWBOARD.DOC file for sample a sample BOARD.CMD file and a discussion
  of why you'll want to change your BOARD.BAT file, door batch files, and
  event batch files.

- NOTE:  Most DOS-based third party programs are going to want to talk to a
  FOSSIL driver.  So in order to use DOS-based doors, file transfer protocols,
  etc, you'll want to load a FOSSIL driver.  If you are using the SIO comm
  driver, this is easy.  Just add DEVICE=\SIO\VX00.SYS to your config.sys
  file.

- A new environment variable has been created called PCBOS2 which is set to Y
  if you are shelled out from PCB-OS2.  You can test for this in a batch file
  using a line such as the following:

      if "%PCBOS2%" == "Y" ... then do whatever

  The quotes are not necessary... they just avoid an error message on the
  screen if PCBOS2 is not declared.

- When PCBoard is shelling out to to either an OS/2 or DOS program, it
  automatically inherits the "environment" that PCBoard is using, including the
  SET PCBDRIVE=, SET PCBDIR=, and other environment variables.

  The OS/2 environment automatically inherits PCBoard's environment when
  shelling.  But PCBoard has to perform a bit of "behind the scenes magic" in
  order to get the environment settings into the DOS environment.  You don't
  normally see this and won't need to care about it.  However, if you have
  PCBoard shell out to an OS/2 program, and then you want to have that OS/2
  program shell out to a DOS program, the DOS program will NOT have the
  environment settings.

       Here is the secret:

  Have the OS/2 application shell out to the DOS application via PCBDOS.BAT.

  For example, if you wanted to run TESTPROG.EXE with command line parameters
  of TESTONE and TESTTWO, you would run it like this:

       PCBDOS.BAT TESTPROG.EXE TESTONE TESTTWO

  The PCBDOS.BAT file will then pass the PCBoard environment settings into the
  environment in which TESTPROG is running.

- NOTE: There is a file called ENV2DOS.DOC file included in the DOCS.ZIP file
  which answers some questions about how to pass environment variables from
  your OS/2 session over to a DOS session.

- There is a tiny .COM program called PCBTITLE.COM.  It is called by
  PCBDOS.BAT and its sole purpose is to change the OS/2 "title bar" to
  indicate the node number of the node that opened the window.  It is only used
  by DOS sessions because the title bar for OS/2 command shells are
  automatically set by PCBoard.

- CMD versus BAT

  In most cases, PCBoard will use either a .BAT file or a .CMD file (the
  former are for DOS batch files, the latter are for OS/2 batch files).
  Deciding which to use should be based on what kind of executable the batch
  file will be running.  For instance, if you create a CMD file that calls
  several DOS programs, you will slow down the system because it will have to
  repeatedly open and close a DOS window to handle each of them.  In other
  words, you can do it... it just won't be efficient.  Instead, you should use
  a .BAT file in that scenario.

  For example, to run an OS/2 door, you go into PCBSetup's DOOR.LST editor and
  answer Y in the OS/2 column.  This will cause PCBoard to create a DOOR.CMD
  when it runs a door.  Therefore, it won't have to create a DOS VDM to run
  the program.  This can also be done if you were to run a DOS program from the
  DOOR.CMD .. but it may be more efficient to leave the OS/2 setting at 'N' and
  run a DOOR.BAT file instead.

  Also, PCBoard will automatically detect and use a .BAT versus .CMD file for
  its shells.  For example, you can continue to use the PCBSZ.BAT and PCBRZ.BAT
  files for your Zmodem file transfers, utilizing the DOS version of Zmodem.
  But if you happen to have an OS/2 module to implement Zmodem, you could
  create PCBSZ.CMD and PCBRZ.CMD files instead.

  PCBoard searches first for a .CMD file and then, only if a .CMD version of
  the file is not found, it will then search for a .BAT file and run it.

  Many of your existing .BAT files (such as $$LOGOFF.BAT) can be run more
  efficiently as .CMD files (assuming they are not running DOS programs).  To
  get PCBoard to run them as a CMD file, then, all you need to do is copy the
  batch file to its CMD counterpart (i.e. $$LOGOFF.BAT becomes $$LOGOFF.CMD).

- Running OS/2 programs with PCBoard:  As mentioned above, in cases where
  PCBoard is shelling out, it will find the appropriate type of batch file to
  execute.  But two more places exist where it is important to allow you to
  decide what type of application to run, these are your doors and events.

  In the DOORS.LST file there is a new OS/2 column.  If you put a Y in that
  column then PCBoard creates a DOOR.CMD file and runs it instead of a DOOR.BAT
  file.  Also, if you have any nodes running the DOS version of PCBoard, these
  doors will be treated as if they were not defined.

  Similarly, in the EVENT.LST file there is also a new OS/2 column.  If you put
  a Y in that column, then only your OS/2 nodes will see the event and when
  the event is executed it will use EVENT.CMD instead of EVENT.BAT.

- There are native OS/2 versions of PCBMoni, PCBPack and USERNET (named
  PCBMONI2.EXE, PCBPACK2.EXE and USERNET2.EXE, respectively). However, no other
  utilities have been converted to native OS/2 versions at this time.  The main
  reason is due to a third party library for which an OS/2 version is not
  available.  We will probably replace this library in the future so a future
  release may contain more native OS/2 utilities.

  Performance-wise, PCBPack for OS/2 is not much different from the DOS
  version because it is primarily spending its time waiting for the hard disk
  and both the DOS and OS/2 versions can "wait" just as effectively. <g>  It
  is included, however, in case it may simplify your event processing by
  using a native OS/2 product.

  Ditto for USERNET2.EXE.  It's inclusion is primarily to make handling events
  inside of .CMD files more seamless.

  PCBMoni for OS/2 is practically unnecessary, due to the existence of the
  PCBoard Control Panel.  However, it is included for those who wish to use
  text-mode applications in a TSHELL environment.

- NOTE:  At this time, an OS/2 version of Zmodem is NOT included in the
  package.  This probably won't change (but could) before v15.22 is released.
  However, PCBoard-for-DOS, including the Zmodem modules, is already very well
  behaved under OS/2 and very "cpu friendly" during file transfers.  So
  although a native OS/2 zmodem will be missed, the existing DOS version will
  run quite effectively with PCBoard-for-OS/2.

  This points out another very important point to keep in mind.  Most of your
  existing DOS applications (doors, utilities, etc) can continue to run,
  unmodified, under PCBoard-for-OS/2!  Except for having to change a few
  batch files (per instructions found in NEWBOARD.DOC), you can convert an
  existing DOS setup over to OS/2 with very little effort.

- Tuning your DOS Applications:

  OS/2 provides a nice set of tuning parameters which you can set for your
  DOS applications by right clicking on an icon and going into the Notebook
  settings.  For instance, you can change the name of the autoexec.bat file, or
  you can set the memory limits, or turn on options to load DOS high or set the
  idle sensitivity, etc.

  This is fine for programs which the WPS launches...  but these settings have
  no effect on programs which PCBoard launches.  However, there is a way to
  configure programs that PCBoard launches.  You can do this in two different
  ways:

  1) You can create a configuration which is specific to a program by creating
     a file by the same name, but with a .OS2 extension, with the settings
     you want specified inside the file.  For example, if PCBoard is shelling
     out to C:\PCB\MYPROG.EXE then it will look for C:\PCB\MYPROG.OS2 to find
     the settings that are specific to MYPROG.EXE.

  2) You can create a "default" configuration file, called PCBDOS.OS2, which
     will hold the settings for any program for which a customized .OS2 file
     is not found.  NOTE:  These are not additive, PCBoard will use one or
     the other but not both, searching first for the customized version then
     for the default file if the customized version is not found.

  The contents of these files look a lot like the DOS Settings page in the
  notebook, with one setting per line.  Here are some examples:

     DOS_HIGH=0
     XMS_MEMORY_LIMIT=0
     EMS_MEMORY_LIMIT=0

  If the above were found in PCBDOS.OS2, and you had no other .OS2 files, then
  every DOS window opened would load DOS low and completely disable any EMS
  or XMS memory.

  If you have a program which requires lots of memory, then you might want to
  create a special .OS2 file for it and set DOS_HIGH=1 and appropriate values
  for the EMS and XMS limits.

  You also might want to include a line such as IDLE_SENSITIVITY=25 for any
  programs which like to hog the CPU.  <g>

  NOTE:  I was unable to get VIDEO_MODE_RESTRICTION=CGA to work, even though
  everything else that I tried worked fine.

  NOTE:  These settings files only pertain to programs which PCBoard shells to
  directly.  For example, your PCBSZ.BAT file (which would be PCBSZ.OS2).
  But it does NOT have any effect on DOS windows which are launched by CMD.EXE,
  so any .CMD files that cause a DOS window to be opened will do so using the
  normal OS/2 defaults for DOS windows.

  NOTE:  The name and location of the settings file for a DOOR would be the
  name of the door + .OS2 and the file would be stored in the same directory
  as the DOOR batch file.  For example, if your DOORS.LST file looks like this:

                                USER DOOR            Charges Per     Path to
     Filename   Pwrd  Sec Login SYS  SYS  Shell Os2 Minute    Use  DOOR Files
    ออออออออออ ออออออ อออ อออออ ออออ ออออ อออออ อออ อออออออออออออ ออออออออออออ
      MYDOOR            0   N    Y    N     Y    N       0      0 C:\PCB\MAIN

  Then PCBoard will look for a file called C:\PCB\MAIN\MYDOOR.OS2 to find the
  settings.

- There is a new OS/2 Settings screen in the configuration options menu:

                                Configuration Options
                                    OS/2 Settings

      Minimize Window Settings      PCBoard   Shell
      ออออออออออออออออออออออออ      อออออออ   อออออ
      $$LOGON/$$LOGOFF Processing  : N      : Y
      External Protocols           : Y      : N
      Door Applications            : Y      : N
      Upload Verification Process  : N      : Y
      File Viewer                  : Y      : N
      PCBQWK & PCBCMPRS Processing : N      : Y
      All Other Shells             : Y      : N

      Priority Settings
      อออออออออออออออออ
      Normal Processing            :   5
      External Protocols           :   0
      PCBQWK & PCBCMPRS Processing :  -2
      Fido Import Processing       :  -5
      Fido Export Processing       :  -5
      All Other Shells             :  -2

  For the "Minimize Window Settings" section of the screen, you can control
  whether PCBoard should "minimize itself" when it shells out, or whether it
  should "minimize the shelled window".  For example, you may want to see what
  is going on while shelled out to a protocol, but you don't necessarily want
  to watch PCBoard compress a mail packet.  So you set PCBoard to minimize the
  shelled window for PCBQWK and PCBCMPRS processing, but on the external
  protocols, it would be nice to remove PCBoard from the screen while the
  protocol is running (to reduce clutter) so you have PCBoard minimize itself.

  In addition, you can set the default priorities for PCBoard's normal
  processing as well as some of the subprocesses.  A setting of 0 allows OS/2
  to completely handle the scheduling of the applications and their threads
  all on its own.  THIS MAY BE THE BEST SETTING OF ALL!  But if you want to
  control the settings yourself, you can enter a value from -31 to 31 to
  adjust the scheduling.  A value of -1 or less reduces the priority, while a
  value of 1 or more increases the priority.

  The example shown above gives PCBoard a slight priority boost while running.
  Meanwhile, things that PCBoard shells out to have their priorities reduced
  a bit so that they can't take time away from active PCBoard processing being
  performed by other nodes.  Finally, the Fido importing and exporting is
  assumed (in this example) to be less important (we don't want to impact the
  performance that an online caller sees) so the priority is reduced even
  further.  NOTE:  THIS IS JUST AN EXAMPLE, IT IS NOT A RECOMMENDATION!

     CAUTION:  When in doubt - leave the settings at 0!

  By messing with priorities, you can really screw up the scheduler.  So use
  this power and flexibility wisely.  If you make a change, be sure to test it
  afterwards so that you understand the implications of the change.

  Further explanation on the above and how everything relates:

   a) "Normal Processing" is the default "boost" in priority that you might
      assign to PCBoard.  If you leave it set to 0, then OS/2 assigns a
      priority of 1 to the process.  This is the normal default for all
      applications and is probably the *best* setting to use unless you have
      a specific reason to change it.

   b) Down below that first line, everything else is "relative".  So if you set
      "Normal Processing" to 5 and then set all of the other values to 0,
      then the entire system will operate with a priority boost of 5.

   c) Given the example up above, then, you can see that external protocols
      would continue to run at a priority boost of 5, while the PCBQWK and
      PCBCMPRS processing would drop down to a boost of only +3 (taking the
      default boost of 5 and subtracting 2 from it).  And Fido's importing and
      exporting would run back at OS/2's default priority setting.

